✔ Tray "Set status" flyout (right-click -> Set status -> Online / Invisible). @started(26-06-05 17:54) @done(26-06-05 17:54) @lasted(43s)
   ✔ Selecting one creates a MANUAL OVERRIDE that lasts until the NEXT scheduled boundary, then the schedule takes back over automatically. @started(26-06-05 17:54)
     Example: schedule says invisible 09:00-16:00. At 10:00 you pick "Online" -> override holds Online until 16:00 (next natural change). After 16:00 the schedule resumes normally (no extra action needed).
   - Persist the override in state file (~/.local/state/sdss/override.json) so it survives restarts/suspend.
   - Tray menu shows a small "Clear override" entry while one is active.
   - Decision logic on every tick: if override active and now < override.expires_at -> apply override.status; else apply schedule.

✔ Tray icon status dot indicator (composite SVG with a small dot in the corner). @started(26-05-15 01:48) @done(26-05-15 01:48) @lasted(43s)
   Split signal: dot COLOR = current effective status, dot BORDER/RING = where that status came from.
   - Dot color (always reflects what Discord/Steam currently are):
       * Green dot = currently online
       * Red dot   = currently invisible / offline
       * Grey dot  = error state (last apply failed - Discord HTTP error, Steam handler missing, etc.)
   - Dot border (orthogonal to color, tells you the source):
       * No ring        = schedule is in charge
       * Blue ring/halo = manual override is active right now
   - Resulting combinations (4 normal states + error):
       * Solid green, no ring -> online, schedule says so
       * Green dot + blue ring -> online because YOU overrode; schedule would say invisible
       * Solid red, no ring -> invisible, schedule says so
       * Red dot + blue ring -> invisible because YOU overrode; schedule would say online
       * Grey dot (with or without ring) -> last apply failed
   - Implementation: keep base icon clean, render the dot + optional ring programmatically (Qt QPainter / Pillow)
     OR ship per-state SVGs in packaging/linux/icons/ (e.g. tray-online.svg, tray-online-override.svg, tray-invisible.svg, tray-invisible-override.svg, tray-error.svg).

~ Network-host mode: a second, mutually exclusive scheduling MODE driven purely by reachability of a host (typically the work PC). @started(26-05-15 01:48)
   IMPLEMENTED (partial): daemon `mode.kind = "network"`, TCP probe (`network_probe.rs`), scheduler integration.
   NOT YET: settings UI (checkbox disabled "coming soon"), ICMP ping, flap retries, full doctor/validate wording.
   When this mode is enabled, it REPLACES the existing schedule entirely:
       * No work window check (timeStart / timeEnd ignored)
       * No holiday check (German holidays ignored)
       * No weekday logic
   Sole rule: if SDSS_WORK_HOST is reachable -> "work hours" -> invisible. If unreachable -> "off hours" -> online.

   - Config (new):
       * SDSS_MODE=schedule | network   (default: schedule, preserves current behavior)
       * SDSS_WORK_HOST=10.0.0.5        (hostname or IP; required when SDSS_MODE=network)
       * SDSS_WORK_PORT=22              (optional; if set, use TCP probe to host:port instead of ICMP ping)
       * SDSS_NET_PROBE_TTL=30          (cache last probe result for N seconds; default 30)
       * SDSS_NET_PROBE_TIMEOUT=1       (seconds for a single probe attempt; default 1)
       * SDSS_NET_PROBE_RETRIES=1       (extra attempts before declaring "down"; default 1, i.e. one retry on first miss to avoid one-packet flaps)
   - Probe behavior:
       * Default: single ICMP ping, 1 packet, SDSS_NET_PROBE_TIMEOUT seconds; fall back automatically to TCP connect on SDSS_WORK_PORT if ping is not permitted (no CAP_NET_RAW, no unprivileged ping).
       * Result cached for SDSS_NET_PROBE_TTL seconds so we are not spamming the network every tick.
       * On flap (UP -> DOWN within a few seconds), require SDSS_NET_PROBE_RETRIES consecutive DOWN probes before flipping to online, to avoid a temporary VPN/Wi-Fi hiccup pulling you online mid-meeting.
   - Manual override still wins over network mode (same expiry rule: lasts until the next natural flip, which here means the next UP<->DOWN transition of the host).
   - doctor must report: SDSS_MODE, probe target, last result, last probe age, whether unprivileged ping works (capabilities) or whether the TCP fallback is in use.
   - validate-config must print plain-language wording for network mode, e.g. "Mode: network. Would set invisible now (work host 10.0.0.5 is reachable)" or "Mode: network. Would set online now (work host 10.0.0.5 is NOT reachable)" - no Discord/Steam HTTP traffic.

~ Suspend/resume + lock/unlock awareness: reapply correct state immediately after system events.
   ✔ Windows: `WM_POWERBROADCAST` resume + 5s settle + apply retry on partial failure (`power.rs`, `scheduler.rs`).
   ✔ Manual override respected across suspend/resume (`state.json` + `override_state.rs`).
   ☐ Lock/unlock events, PrepareForSleep hook (`SDSS_ON_SUSPEND`), logind DBus — Linux-era spec, not ported.

✔ Doctor++ live tray state (not urgent — use journalctl / tray terminal logs today):
   ✔ `sdss doctor` reads live `StatusSnapshot` from daemon via named pipe when `sdssd` is running.
   ✔ Rotating NDJSON logs under `state/logs/` + Logs tab + `sdss logs` (replaces journalctl for Windows).

✔ Tray single-instance: only one `sdssd` at a time (Windows mutex in `main.rs`). Second start exits cleanly.

